#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166634 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/id #uuid "e8353dba-48d7-5f4f-8ba5-97e4e22b6e44" :question/math-academy-id "e-9050" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\, \\rm{ m/s}$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\sin^2 x \\,\\rm{m/s}^2.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ what differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/f187eff194f136838b67ab12b44f8f6882eafb59885a5c3f440d41492def4a42.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\sin^2 x$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = -\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-{sin}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = -\\sin^2 x.\\qquad \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(v^2) = 2v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(\\ast)$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = -\\sin^2 x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166635 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/id #uuid "49e02115-6211-564b-8221-d5835a98a064" :question/math-academy-id "e-9051" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x \\,\\rm{m},$ its velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s}$ and its acceleration is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\, \\rm{m/s}^2.$ If $v = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when $x = 2 \\, \\textrm m,$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x = 1 \\, \\textrm m.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/4fdd70a4ca80965c0adce9c60be36b0712852b662cff69f9733f50c739fd1b32.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = 2x + e^{x-2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = 2x + e^{x-2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+{e}^{x-2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2 = x^2 + e^{x-2} + C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when $x = 2 \\, \\textrm m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={2}^{2}+{e}^{2-2}+C \\\\ 8 & =4+1+C \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x) \\gt 0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{2\\left(x^2 + e^{x-2} + 3\\right)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x = 1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{2({1}^{2}+{e}^{1-2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2(4+{e}^{-1})} \\\\  & ≈2.96\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166644 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/id #uuid "80b653d0-5dff-5621-86ee-e1fd336ad740" :question/math-academy-id "e-9455" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\geq 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\textrm m,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/eca36626e6a0cfb30d293e684483c37ddce6f18d4004c3f77258e9663565d8d7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} =-\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = -\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when $x=0\\, \\textrm{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =\\frac{2}{0+1}+K \\\\ 2 & =2+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{x+1} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x) > 0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x) = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}} \\\\ \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2 \\\\ \\int \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(x+1)^{3/2} & =2t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+1)^{3/2} & =3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = \\dfrac{3C_1}{2}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{3/2} =3t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{3/2} & =3t+1 \\\\ x+1 & =(3t+1)^{2/3} \\\\ x & =(3t+1)^{2/3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "832f5079-56a5-5633-a36a-abcaa350c6f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2}) = 6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163437} {:answer/id #uuid "ece83403-1738-5762-a14b-2869fedf272b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dx} = 6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163438} {:answer/id #uuid "032ae97f-c542-562e-bb45-fad43b5fc7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(v^{2}) =-6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163439} {:answer/id #uuid "342bdb71-912c-58bd-a61f-2bf43bf15640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dx} =-6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163440} {:answer/id #uuid "366f26c4-1e76-5037-be3b-fe4e333ae3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2}) =-6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "366f26c4-1e76-5037-be3b-fe4e333ae3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2}) =-6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163441} :answer-field/id #uuid "5043cace-708e-584e-9f93-2239e526e2c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cde0f0e6-e59d-5767-8e42-f1f093f582b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-108010" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $6{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/52fc197612dac35373ecebf771b8444505902b5c6e00c7c55d2b21dab4809136.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $6{x}^{3}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-6{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-6{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=6{x}^{3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-6{x}^{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ceb8256-c17a-53e1-ac22-32f6f795df58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t-\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314069} {:answer/id #uuid "1d18169b-8026-562c-a919-850efef7b461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314070} {:answer/id #uuid "b177d108-a207-50df-9c89-8b321579c5ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314071} {:answer/id #uuid "f9552b99-b391-5ece-9344-829c730e0c50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314072} {:answer/id #uuid "01416141-3538-5a15-97ee-0eb2e61d5c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi +t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d18169b-8026-562c-a919-850efef7b461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314070} :answer-field/id #uuid "01399d4a-955d-5d24-b875-b8a046ac00c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1494f900-5c6d-561b-9ba9-7d9115f8d044" :question/math-academy-id "q-122312" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=(1+4{x}^{2})\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t$ where $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{8}].$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/59c4a51d419b6e42633579b687cde16a2842895c589f818942c10aa304bc98a7.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+4{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡2x & =t+K \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+C) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=0,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30dda7a5-4016-5e40-9224-3c6279cdd41c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314076} {:answer/id #uuid "db3b5ecf-1216-57f7-94c9-70c9b7c34f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314077} {:answer/id #uuid "7c730bf0-872c-5722-bdec-13ef6599263d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314078} {:answer/id #uuid "2096a1f6-beb6-516f-98b7-c133fda87158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314079} {:answer/id #uuid "762a12c9-05cb-5020-8b40-db0ca1b94098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2096a1f6-beb6-516f-98b7-c133fda87158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314079} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ca2bad5-eebc-557a-a58f-638f93fcb85c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05e75a3d-3e2e-5128-ae1f-6d92d30f38f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122313" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/00da8cdff7814df7368ff00924360db24a93934c5ae1c87ceb5daa6b00df37a7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0f9e10f-81f8-5eb5-a68a-d90a070f19b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314083} {:answer/id #uuid "9fdc8b3d-cc9f-5b98-ab40-99bb47c0eb58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314084} {:answer/id #uuid "02ac20cd-bd74-5742-8b19-0ec599a24f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314085} {:answer/id #uuid "d1def38b-cfcd-58df-8efc-8af24f5e934a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314086} {:answer/id #uuid "24cf1ab2-5761-5250-bf0d-c8c08c4244a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fdc8b3d-cc9f-5b98-ab40-99bb47c0eb58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314084} :answer-field/id #uuid "4759492e-6d28-5a9e-b0c3-df20068a67e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fca9354-5321-5542-b5b1-39625f1faa01" :question/math-academy-id "q-122315" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(3{e}^{x}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/785776cee4f82c5bfa5e86b5b1cb746b7c80f2d702fb52f67055a60bdff249ae.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(3{e}^{x}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=3{e}^{x}+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3{e}^{x}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5acf374f-291a-5639-a46b-7acac2eef5a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314090} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f8ec42-40f1-55df-a2d2-6d0cfd24fbfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314091} {:answer/id #uuid "01be0c81-03f1-5b16-afe3-01d09fe19fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314092} {:answer/id #uuid "3c860876-8077-5d8a-b490-1124115ea5ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314093} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc2e683-94fa-5538-ac1d-59eb33666c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8f8ec42-40f1-55df-a2d2-6d0cfd24fbfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314091} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1a84703-3c19-5a54-b80b-5eeb8b58eb55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f818b71-1c62-567d-b5ef-4300712765ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-122325" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)={e}^{-2x}\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/8faba5a783797f7025f51ec1ae587cb68355dff7138278689346c2f713275562.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{-2x}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int {e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x} & =t+K \\\\ {e}^{2x} & =2t+C \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+C) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56fe543e-e026-5266-a271-24932d9b1e27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314097} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4a2c70-469e-5ffc-87f6-84c5cbbc4d59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{3{e}^{x-3}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314098} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3928b8-a77e-5319-b69a-404239609725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=3{e}^{x-3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314099} {:answer/id #uuid "d7cf0967-6ed2-591f-b85d-a076aa8110f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)={e}^{x-3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314100} {:answer/id #uuid "b253ed67-676c-5100-b57b-d40fec708aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{{e}^{x-3}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56fe543e-e026-5266-a271-24932d9b1e27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314097} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea53bf7f-7eb2-545c-a38e-16d9d8e43c05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b97a4f6-ee90-5fc9-94b5-f52f95caa841" :question/math-academy-id "q-122337" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{x-3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $3\\,m$ when its velocity is $2\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/164c90893a07ae9657e90b6f2b25e53c9c4927b1f4a076429ba0521a98377f24.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{x-3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{x-3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={e}^{x-3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={e}^{x-3}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=3\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={e}^{x-3}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & ={e}^{3-3}+C \\\\ 2 & =1+C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={e}^{x-3}+1 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({e}^{x-3}+1) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8540e57d-b048-5ab3-aeaa-e7c48698f013" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{2t+1}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314132} {:answer/id #uuid "5f73179e-8a65-5462-9098-cbd299e605f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314133} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9d27b1-016c-540e-9806-3c428ea3a9f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(3t+2)^{3}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314134} {:answer/id #uuid "c168ec6d-57b5-5f35-abb9-4c56118eb100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(12t-1)^{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314135} {:answer/id #uuid "f391d876-9bc8-51c8-8822-3545affef68b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{t-8}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f73179e-8a65-5462-9098-cbd299e605f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314133} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba6e123a-1fab-58ba-a491-c61f45ab4aff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b41d621c-b89a-58ab-8c66-2280c7bf01d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-122349" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=1\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/141c6dd6c91841238298b86fac1063c76faeb7cd103e13d2723f6ac05ed58f50.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=1\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(0+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\ (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x+2)^{3} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+2)^{3} & =12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=8,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{3}=12t+8.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{3} & =12t+8 \\\\ x+2 & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
